#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background (Fortran) {#background-fortran}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The nuclear shell model treats a nucleus as a few *valence* nucleons moving "
"in a small set of single-particle orbitals above an inert *core*, "
"interacting through an empirical two-body force fitted to measured spectra. "
"Its computational cost is combinatorial: the basis is every way of "
"distributing the valence protons and neutrons over the available states, and"
" this growth limits the model spaces accessible to exact diagonalization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pooled sample-based quantum diagonalization (pooled SQD) "
"[\\[1\\]](#references) splits that problem in two. A quantum circuit is used"
" only to *propose* which basis states matter. It is measured in the "
"computational basis, and each measured bitstring names one Slater "
"determinant. The Hamiltonian is then built and diagonalized classically in "
"the span of those determinants. Because the classical step is an exact "
"diagonalization inside a subspace, it returns a variational upper bound on "
"the true ground-state energy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Noise changes *which* determinants the circuit proposes, but it does not "
"enter the classical Hamiltonian. A shot that violates a conserved quantity "
"is discarded, and a shot that survives is a legitimate basis vector however "
"it was produced. Noise therefore costs subspace quality, not correctness. "
"Nuclear structure provides several exact quantum numbers for this check: a "
"physical determinant must carry the right number of valence protons *and* "
"valence neutrons, the right total angular-momentum projection $M_J$, and the"
" right parity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Fortran application uses the same register as the Python example. Every "
"qubit is one $m$-scheme single-particle state $(n, \\ell, j, m_j, t_z)$, and"
" $|1\\rangle$ means occupied. Protons come first, then neutrons; within a "
"species, orbitals follow the order of the interaction file; within an "
"orbital, $m_j$ descends. The $^{20}\\mathrm{Ne}$ example here uses this "
"24-qubit $sd$-shell register.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Hamiltonian is a one-body term plus a two-body interaction,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = \\sum_{p} \\varepsilon_p\\, a_p^\\dagger a_p\n"
"  + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\sum_{pqrs} \\langle pq \\| rs \\rangle\\, a_p^\\dagger a_q^\\dagger a_s a_r ,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $p,q,r,s$ label $m$-scheme states. The interaction file tabulates two-"
"body matrix elements in the $J$-coupled basis of orbitals, and the "
"application recovers each $m$-scheme element $\\langle pq \\| rs \\rangle$ "
"by Clebsch-Gordan recoupling. The regression test in [Check the workflow "
"locally](#check-the-workflow-locally) confirms that this produces the same "
"Hamiltonian as the Python example.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Fortran workflow {#the-fortran-workflow}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Reference determinant.** Fill the lowest single-particle-energy $(+m_j, "
"-m_j)$ pairs of each species, which gives $M_J = 0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Excitation pool.** Generate the single and double excitations of the "
"reference allowed by angular-momentum coupling, rank them by a second-order "
"perturbation-theory score, $V^2/|\\Delta|$, and seed each with its two-level"
" mixing angle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Circuit ensemble.** Each circuit prepares the reference and applies the "
"next slice of the ranked pool: Givens rotations for single excitations and "
"qubit-excitation gates for double excitations. With the default of 16 "
"excitations per circuit, 11 circuits cover the whole double-excitation pool "
"for $^{20}\\mathrm{Ne}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Sample.** Transpile each circuit and sample it on a QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Filter.** Keep only shots with the right proton number, neutron number, "
"$M_J$, and parity, and discard the rest.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Diagonalize.** Build the Hamiltonian in the span of the unique kept "
"determinants, pooled across all circuits, and diagonalize it with LAPACK to "
"get a variational upper bound. Alternatively, diagonalize each circuit's "
"samples separately.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each step maps to a source file in the Fortran directory:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Step | Source |\n"
"|---|---|\n"
"| Read the interaction and recouple to the $m$-scheme | `physics/usdb_reader.f90`, `physics/clebsch_gordan.f90`, `physics/orbital_registry.f90` |\n"
"| Rank and seed the excitation pool | `nuclear_pool.f90`, `nuclear_selection.f90` |\n"
"| Build the circuits | `circuit/nuclear_ansatz.f90`, `nuclear_gates.f90` |\n"
"| Sample, filter, and diagonalize | `circuit/nuclear_shell_driver.f90`, `physics/symmetry_filter.f90`, `physics/exact_solver.f90` |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Fortran workflow differs from the Python example in several choices, "
"such as how it picks the reference determinant and how it handles samples "
"with the wrong symmetry. [Differences from the Python example](#differences-"
"from-the-python-example) lists them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements (Fortran) {#requirements-fortran}"
msgstr ""

